"## FEVER fact verification task: definition of evidence retrieval from Wikipedia, dataset construction, and evaluation metrics (evidence precision/recall/F1, label accuracy)\n# FEVER: Fact Extraction and VERification — Task, Dataset, and Metrics\n\n## 1. Task definition and evidence retrieval\n\nFEVER [1] is defined over a processed English Wikipedia corpus. Given a claim *c*, a system must (a) retrieve the Wikipedia evidence for the claim and (b) decide its truth value, performing three sub-tasks:\n\n1. **Document retrieval** – identify the Wikipedia pages containing evidence.\n2. **Evidence (sentence) retrieval** – select the specific sentences within those pages.\n3. **Label prediction (aggregation)** – classify the claim as **SUPPORTS**, **REFUTES**, or **NOT ENOUGH INFO** (NEI).\n\n**Formal evidence definition [1]:** Evidence for a claim is a set of *evidence groups*; each group is a set of (page, sentence-line) tuples. A group is *sufficient* if the claim can be inferred from it alone; a claim is SUPPORTS/REFUTES if at least one sufficient group exists in the gold evidence, and NEI if no such evidence exists in Wikipedia. Retrieval therefore operates at **(page title, sentence index) granularity**, not free-text spans.\n\n**Corpus [1]:** the 2016-12-21 English Wikipedia dump, with tables, infoboxes, lists and other boilerplate removed; pages split into passages and then sentences (~5.4M evidence candidates).\n\n## 2. Dataset construction [1]\n\nClaims were crowdsourced by mutating sentences drawn from intro sections of popular (highly edited) Wikipedia pages, using operations with intended labels:\n\n- **SUPPORTED:** copy/paraphrase of the source sentence (meaning preserved).\n- **REFUTED:** entity substitution, negation, or numeral/date perturbation.\n- **NOT ENOUGH INFO:** substitution with an entity/topic not covered by the page, so evidence does not exist.\n\nQuality-control pipeline:\n- **Evidence annotation:** for REFUTED/NEI claims, separate annotators located the minimal supporting/refuting sentences (and for NEI, searched to confirm no evidence exists); evidence can span multiple sentences/pages (multi-hop).\n- **Evidence check / adversarial verification:** an independent annotator, shown only the claim plus the annotated evidence, must reproduce the intended label; claims failing this check were discarded or re-annotated.\n\n**Statistics [1]:** ~185,445 claims; standard split ≈ **145,433 train / 19,958 dev / 6,666 test** (test labels withheld for the shared task; the dev pool includes labelled and unlabelled portions used in the FEVER-2018 shared task [2]). Train label distribution: ≈80,353 SUPPORTS (55%), ≈36,061 REFUTES (25%), ≈29,019 NEI (20%).\n\n## 3. Evaluation metrics\n\nOfficial scoring via the `fever-scorer` tool [2][3]:\n\n- **Document retrieval:** page-level **Recall@k** (typically k=5) — an upper bound for downstream evidence retrieval.\n- **Evidence precision / recall / F1:** computed per claim over (page, line) tuples with exact matching, against the union of gold evidence groups:\n  - P = |Ê ∩ E| / |Ê|, R = |Ê ∩ E| / |E|, F1 = 2PR/(P+R), macro-averaged over claims. **NEI claims are excluded** from evidence metrics (no gold evidence exists for them).\n- **Label accuracy:** fraction of claims whose 3-way predicted label matches the gold label.\n- **FEVER score (primary end-to-end metric):** a claim counts as correct **only if the label is correct AND the submitted evidence is sufficient**, operationalized as evidence **F1 ≥ θ with θ = 0.05** in the official scorer [1][3]. This penalizes systems that guess the right label from wrong/insufficient evidence; for NEI claims only the label is checked.\n\n## 4. Reference numbers\n\n- Original baseline pipeline (Elasticsearch/TF-IDF retrieval + learned sentence selection + neural aggregator) [1]: document Recall@5 ≈ 0.87, evidence/label pipeline FEVER score ≈ **31%** on dev.\n- Best FEVER-2018 shared task system (UNC; multi-hop attention networks) [2][4]: ≈ **69.4%** test FEVER score.\n\n**Key references:** [1] Thorne et al., *FEVER: A Large-Scale Dataset for Fact Extraction and VERification*, NAACL 2018; [2] Thorne et al., *The FEVER-2018 Shared Task*, NAACL 2019; [3] official `fever-scorer`/baseline code (sheffieldnlp/fever-baselines, github.com); [4] Nie et al., *Combining Fact Extraction and Claim Verification with Multi-hop Attention Networks*, ACL 2019.\n\n*Note: figures are reported from the canonical papers; exact split sizes and baseline decimals should be verified against [1] and the released data files if precision is critical.*\n\n---\n\n## Passage retrieval and sentence-level evidence extraction methods from Wikipedia for claim verification: TF-IDF/BM25, dense retrieval, neural ranking, evidence aggregation pipelines\nI'll research this topic systematically, starting with searches on the core retrieval and evidence extraction methods for claim verification.\n\n---\n\n## Benchmarks and systems for Wikipedia-based claim verification 2018-2025: FEVER, FEVER 2.0 adversarial, AVeriTeC, SciFact, CLEF CheckThat, multilingual extensions\nI'll research this topic systematically, starting with the foundational FEVER benchmark and its evolution.\n\n---\n\n## Use of Wikipedia structure (entity linking, hyperlinks, tables, revision history, citations) as evidence for fact-checking and claim support\nI'll research this topic systematically, looking at how different Wikipedia structural elements have been used in fact-checking research.\n\n---\n\n## Programmatic access to Wikipedia article content for research: MediaWiki API, dumps, DBpedia, Wikidata, and tools used to extract and annotate passages\n# Programmatic Access to Wikipedia for Research\n\n## Comparison at a glance\n\n| Channel | Best for | Format | Freshness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MediaWiki Action API | Targeted queries, revisions, metadata | JSON (wikitext/HTML/extracts) | Live |\n| REST API | Summaries, rendered HTML, pageviews | JSON / HTML | Live |\n| Dumps | Full-corpus, reproducible research | XML (wikitext), JSON, SQL | Snapshot (~2×/month) |\n| DBpedia | RDF triples, entity linking | RDF / SPARQL | Release-based |\n| Wikidata | Structured facts, cross-lingual entity IDs | SPARQL / JSON | Live + dumps |\n\n## 1. MediaWiki APIs\n- **Action API** (`https://{lang}.wikipedia.org/w/api.php`): `action=query&prop=revisions&rvslots=main&rvprop=content` returns raw wikitext; `prop=extracts` (TextExtracts) returns plain-text extracts; `action=parse` renders wikitext to HTML. Batch up to 50 titles per request (500 with bot rights), use `continue` for pagination, set a descriptive `User-Agent`, and use `maxlag` for polite crawling [1].\n- **REST API** (`/api/rest_v1/` and the newer `api.wikimedia.org`): `page/summary/{title}`, `page/html/{title}`, `page/mobile-sections/`, plus the **Pageviews API** for per-article traffic [2].\n- **Real-time channels**: EventStreams (Kafka-based SSE feed of edits) [17]; **ORES/Lift Wing** for ML-scored article quality/damaging/topic annotations [18].\n- **Client libraries**: pywikibot, mwclient, mwapi, wptools (Python); WikipediR/WikidataR (R); MediaWikiToolkit (Java).\n\n## 2. Dumps (dumps.wikimedia.org)\n- `pages-articles.xml.bz2` — current wikitext of all articles (multistream bz2 with index files for random access by page ID); `pages-meta-history` — full revision history (7z, multi-part); SQL files for `pagelinks`, `categorylinks`, `redirect`, etc.; `abstract.xml` — intro extracts; **CirrusSearch JSON dumps** — pre-processed full-text + metadata for search [3].\n- **Wikidata dumps**: `latest-all.json.gz` plus per-entity-type shards (items/properties/lexemes) [8].\n- Cadence for large wikis is roughly twice monthly; always cite the dump date for reproducibility.\n- Offline mirror option: Kiwix ZIM files.\n\n## 3. DBpedia\n- Extracts structured RDF from Wikipedia (infoboxes, abstracts, categories, links); query via the public **SPARQL endpoint** (`https://dbpedia.org/sparql`, ~60 s timeout); releases distributed via the **Databus** [4].\n- **DBpedia Spotlight**: REST service for entity recognition + disambiguation against DBpedia URIs (annotate/candidate/disambiguate endpoints) [5].\n- **NIF corpora**: article text serialized in the NLP Interchange Format with character offsets — useful for passage-level NLP experiments [6].\n- Caveat: community-edition extraction lags Wikipedia; coverage/quality varies by release.\n\n## 4. Wikidata\n- **SPARQL Query Service** (`query.wikidata.org`, Blazegraph): 60 s timeout, label service for multilingual labels; the standard route for entity sets, sitelinks, and graph queries [7].\n- **Action API**: `action=wbgetentities&sites=enwiki&titles=...` maps Wikipedia titles ↔ QIDs and returns claims; `Special:EntityData/Q42.json` gives content-negotiated RDF/JSON.\n- **Dumps**: JSON entity dumps [8]; processing tools include WikidataToolkit (Java), qwikidata/kgtk (Python/TSV pipelines).\n- QIDs are the de facto stable entity keys for cross-lingual alignment of Wikipedia content.\n\n## 5. Passage extraction & annotation tooling\n**Dump → text**\n- **wikiextractor**: dump XML → `<doc>`-tagged plain text [10]; **gensim WikiCorpus**: streaming tokenized articles [11]; **mwparserfromhell / wikitextparser**: surgical wikitext parsing (templates, refs, links) [9]; Parsoid or `action=parse` for HTML + boilerplate stripping.\n\n**Ready-made passage corpora & indexes** (fixed snapshots, widely used for retrieval/QA)\n- **DPR** `psgs_w100.tsv`: 21M 100-word passages from the 2016-12-21 dump [12].\n- **KILT knowledge source**: 5.9M articles, 2019-08-01 snapshot, provenance-aware [13].\n- **Pyserini/Anserini**: prebuilt BM25 and dense (DPR/Contriever/BGE) indexes over these corpora [14].\n- **Hugging Face `wikimedia/wikipedia`**: per-language streaming datasets (id/url/title/text) [15].\n- Downstream benchmark corpora built from Wikipedia passages: SQuAD, Natural Questions, HotpotQA, FEVER, TriviaQA.\n\n**Entity linking / annotation**\n- DBpedia Spotlight [5]; **TagMe**; **WAT** (Slotted Annotation Tool) for Wikipedia-concept annotation [16]; GENRE/MGENRE and BLINK (autoregressive/dense entity retrieval); REL; spaCy entity linker with a DBpedia KB. Wikipedia-derived NER data: **WikiAnn**.\n\n## Practical guidance\n- **Reproducibility**: API content is live and mutable — pin to dated dumps (or record page IDs + revision IDs) and Wikidata QIDs; report the snapshot date in papers.\n- **Scale**: use dumps (or Wikimedia Enterprise, a supported high-volume API with structured HTML and snapshots) [20] rather than hammering the API; respect rate limits and the User-Agent policy [1].\n- **Licensing**: Wikipedia text is CC BY-SA (3.0 historically; the 2023 Terms of Use update moved new revisions to CC BY-SA 4.0) — attribution and share-alike obligations apply to redistribution [19].\n\n## Sources\n[1] MediaWiki Action API docs — mediawiki.org/wiki/API:Main_page\n[2] Wikimedia REST API — en.wikipedia.org/api/rest_v1/; api.wikimedia.org\n[3] Wikimedia dumps — dumps.wikimedia.org; meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data_dumps\n[4] DBpedia & SPARQL — dbpedia.org; dbpedia.org/sparql\n[5] DBpedia Spotlight — dbpedia-spotlight.org\n[6] DBpedia NIF corpora — databus.dbpedia.org (NIF datasets)\n[7] Wikidata Query Service manual — wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:SPARQL_query_service\n[8] Wikidata database download — wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Database_download\n[9] mwparserfromhell — github.com/earwig/mwparserfromhell\n[10] wikiextractor — github.com/attardi/wikiextractor\n[11] gensim WikiCorpus — radimrehurek.com/gensim\n[12] DPR passages — github.com/facebookresearch/DPR\n[13] KILT — github.com/facebookresearch/KILT\n[14] Pyserini prebuilt indexes — github.com/castorini/pyserini\n[15] wikimedia/wikipedia dataset — huggingface.co/datasets/wikimedia/wikipedia\n[16] WAT annotation tool — Piccinno & Pizzato, \"WAT: A Tool for Wikipedia-based Annotation\"\n[17] EventStreams — stream.wikimedia.org; wikitech.wikimedia.org/wiki/EventStreams\n[18] Lift Wing (ORES) — wikitech.wikimedia.org/wiki/Machine_Learning/LiftWing\n[19] Wikimedia Terms of Use / licensing — foundation.wikimedia.org/wiki/Policy:Terms_of_Use\n[20] Wikimedia Enterprise — enterprise.wikimedia.com"